 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section dialect
  (doc "Construction sugar at the reader plus the record vocabulary it
       unlocks. Everything else honors the Forth law: syntax stays
       frozen elsewhere, the dictionary grows, and any syntax addition
       must FOLD into what already exists or go FINDINGS.  IMPLEMENTED
       v0.1: brackets + number literals + let at the reader, records +
       list-filter in the stdlib v1.1 rows, the todo bridge green.
       v0.2: keyed literals {...} + the shipped todo prelude.")
  (status implemented)
  (subsection laws
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      L1 PARENS COMPUTE, BRACKETS CONSTRUCT. [...] is the data form:
         a term that builds structure and cannot evaluate further
         than its own elements; (...) stays computation. Brackets are
         reserved OUTSIDE this habitat too: the nopales reader must
         keep them FORBIDDEN so the paren-habitat vs bracket-calculus
         tell survives the F3 bridge (the stdlib pin, kept here as
         law).
      L2 DESUGAR IS A TOTAL READER FOLD. Every sugar form expands at
         parse time into a term the TODAY grammar accepts. The
         expansion is CHECKED, never trusted: form and hand-written
         expansion must compile to the identical tree and agree on
         step counts (12.1). Desugared nodes inherit the sugar
         form's span, so IR paths coincide with the hand form's and
         diagnostics/provenance do not move.
      L3 NUMBER LITERALS ARE LITERALS. Decimal and -decimal atoms
         read as Tree_lit of the CANONICAL int (stdlib conventions
         law 5), exactly as %ternary pins read: not shadowable, not
         identifiers, canonical at read (-0 = 0 = fork false nil).
         No float, no hex, no widths. Bare digit runs are a lexical
         ERROR today, so the positive flip is purely additive; the
         negative flip reserves the -digits shape, which IS a small
         grammar change - audited (12.2), and -digits-then-letters
         stays an identifier.
      L4 FORM HEADS RESERVE. lambda/prim/runtime reserve their head
         atoms today; let joins that set, and letrec joins it ONLY
         if it ships (L5). Non-head atoms stay free: a word named
         'let' remains a legal def and identifier; only the form-head
         position goes reserved, so sources are audited anyway
         (12.2).
      L5 VOCABULARY BEFORE KEYWORDS. A form exists only on a
         CITATION from the port (the stdlib's measured-need
         standard); where a stdlib def can carry the job, the def
         wins. letrec and every further sugar idea (patterns,
         operators, more literal domains, multi-binding letrec) are
         GATED on that citation - not shipped, not sketched here.
      L6 SUGAR IS PRICED. Every form lands with its corpus twin
         (12.1), its construction-triage and compiled-size rows in
         the fuel table (12.4), and the F5 watch extended to
         desugared output: sugar must not multiply compiled size
         against its hand-written twin. |))))
  (subsection forms
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      BRACKETS (pinned first move). [a b c] desugars at the reader
         to (pair a (pair b (pair c 0))) - the cons-applications the
         compiler accepts today; [] is 0. Construction cost is
         claimed ZERO triage (pair is Leaf: apply wrappers are not
         rule firings; AGENTS 4); 12.4 measures the claim and 12.1
         pins desugar identity against the hand pair-chain.
      NUMBERS (pinned second move). 42 reads as the Tree_lit of the
         int-law-5 tree; -7 likewise (sign flipped). The DECODER is
         a common/ module beside Tuna.Cstr - reader, tests, probes
         and the stdlib gen pipeline share ONE codec for law-5 ints
         (the seeded stdlib executes no host code, so the encoding
         has to live as code here); the stdlib rows stay the
         semantic REFERENCE for it (differential-pinned like the
         math prims: literal 2 must agree with int-add int-one
         int-one).
      LET (v0.1, cited by the port). (let ((x a) (y b)) B)
         desugars to ((lambda (x) ((lambda (y) B) b)) a): sequential
         right-nesting; lambda-param shadowing gives the scoping
         for free. Reserved as a head form (L4). If the finished
         port never uses it, drop it before landing - do not ship
         unused grammar.
      LETREC (gated, default OUT of v0.1). The single-binding
         shape (letrec ((f E)) B), where E may mention f,
         desugars to (let ((f ((rec-fix (lambda (f) E))))) B);
         multiple or mutual bindings are out of scope (L5). GATE:
         ships only if the port measurably trips threading the
         rec-fix idiom by hand. LAW when it ships: dialect keywords
         may lean on STDLIB NAMES; resolution order stands (param >
         identity > sabralib > builtins), shadowing wins, collisions
         recorded not prevented.
      Strings need NO new form: string literals are already reader
         terms (Tuna.Cstr) and ride the one list law; text payloads
         belong to byte-values + the prim world per byte-values law
         4.
      KEYED LITERALS (v0.2). {:name value ...} desugars at the reader
         to the bracket list of [key value] pairs the record
         vocabulary already reads: {:state x :title y} becomes
         [[key-state x] [key-title y]] (each pair is
         (pair key (pair value 0))). A :name key resolves to the
         dictionary name key-name exactly as a bare atom would
         (scope > dictionary > builtin), so the SCHEMA stays data and
         the reader knows no fields. Total fold, same 12.1 equality
         law: the form and its hand-written [key-value value] twin
         compile to the identical tree and steps. Reserved shape: { }
         and the :key token join the reader grammar; a :key outside a
         keyed literal is a normal (if usually unbound) atom. |))))
  (subsection records
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      THE CONVENTION the stdlib deferred out of v1 ('record/assoc
      conventions ... adding one is a book change citing measured
      need' - this chapter's port is that citation): a record IS A
      LIST of (key value) TWO-LISTS. The ONE list law carries it;
      no second list convention is introduced, ever. Constructors
      compose with the sugar: [k v] is (pair k (pair v 0)); a
      record literal is a bracket list of those pairs.  An ENTRY's
      value is `first (second kv)` (the `second` is the singleton
      tail `[value]`) - the one shape worth pinning in prose because
      the natural mistake, `second kv`, hands the tail list instead.

      KEY DOMAIN is decided at MAKE TIME by the fuel table (12.4),
      ties break to TAGS: law-4 small-nat keys (tags are what nats
      are for; constructor cost zero triage; they collide with
      bool/option readings by design - recorded, not prevented)
      versus cstr string keys (greppable, but pure string ops price
      like unary bloat per byte-values law 4, so strings must EARN
      the row on measured numbers, not on readability taste).
      LANDED: tags (small-nat keys) - the todo port keys state/title/
      who/when by small nat; no string-keyed lookup was needed, so
      the string rows never had to earn a place.

      VALUES stay open per the ordinary conventions (strings,
      ints, whole trees-as-programs all legal fields).

      V0.2 KEY DOMAIN MEASURED (the fuel-table decision this law
      demanded, 4-field record, v0 runtime steps on a clean run):
      small-nat tags 2,051; int-literal keys 4,418; cstr string keys
      27,817.  Ties broke to tags and the numbers confirmed it:
      tags are 2.2x cheaper than int keys and 13.6x cheaper than
      strings.  So the shipped schema (below) keys every field by a
      law-4 small nat.  (The spreadsheet's earlier '10x' line was a
      hand-count; the measured ordering is tags < int < string, and
      the win over ints is smaller than the win over strings.)

      DEFS land as stdlib v1.1 ADDITIVE rows through the seeded
      pipeline unchanged (gen_defs -> freeze -> manifest bump ->
      generated embed; seeding stays skip-if-exists additive; a
      product hash that changes lands as new hashes, history
      untouched). NAMED BY THE PORT, never speculative: the
      candidates are rec-get (option answer per stdlib law 3),
      rec-upd, rec-has, plus whatever the todo port actually
      reaches for (filters, sorts) - the port writes the manifest
      additions, the manifest does not anticipate it. Every def
      property-pinned, manifest-rowed, corpus-entered: verify-8
      stays the referee and must stay green over 53+v1.1 rows.
      LANDED (61 defs total, 53 v1 rows untouched): none, some,
      rec-get, rec-has, rec-val (option unwrap for arithmetic
      consumers), rec-replace, rec-upd (replace-or-append), and
      list-filter (the port's one list reach).  none/some are the
      law-3 option alias defs the record code reads - added because
      the port spells `(some x)` rather than raw `(%0 x)`.
      |))))
  (subsection todo-prelude
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      THE SHIPPED SCHEMA (v0.2, 17 additive rows; MANIFEST 76).
      The missing maturity the todo probe hit was a NAMING layer, so
      the chapter ships one - ordinary defs, no new reader beyond the
      keyed literal, no host change:

        key-state key-title key-who key-when   small-nat field tags
        todo-open todo-done                     the two states
        todo-state todo-title todo-who todo-when   rec-val accessors
        todo-entry                              record out of a
                                                tree/list entry
        todo-item                               the 4-field record
        todo-created                            tree/put a fresh item
        todo-flip                               open<->done in-calculus
        todo-open-count                         fold open items

      Zero-cost where it can be: an accessor like (todo-state r) is a
      def whose body is (rec-val key-state r); it bracket-abstracts to
      the SAME tree as the raw access (verified: identical hash, 2,051
      v0 steps when the key is a small nat), so the vocabulary is
      readability with no runtime tax.  todo-created is the whole
      create call site; todo-flip is the pure state transition.  The
      prelude is a CONVENTION, not a cage: an identity can shadow any
      todo-* name (resolution order), and a product with a different
      schema defines its own key-* tags - the reader never learns the
      fields.

      WHAT A PRODUCT NOW WRITES (the whole point):
        (lambda (path title who)
          (prim "tree/put" path
            {:state todo-open :title title :who who :when 1727000000}))
      - one effect, one readable record literal, 64 runtime steps,
      one journaled tree/put, replay-verified.  The calculus is still
      underneath; nobody writing this had to see it.

      GATED, NOT SHIPPED (L5): a `:name` field-accessor head form
      ((:state r) for (todo-state r)) was considered and DROPPED - the
      accessor defs carry it with zero new grammar, and the port never
      tripped; ship the keyword only on a measured citation.  Same for
      operators and further literal domains. |))))
  (subsection todo-bridge
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      THE DOGFOOD - the 2026-10-02 review's named product candidate,
      done in-calculus: the todo board's ITEM LOGIC in sabra against
      real boundary effects.

      SHAPE: a sabra program holding a granted namespace (default
      todo-cal, disjoint from the host page's todo/ flat ids, so
      the OCaml board and the sabra board coexist without artifact
      noise in each other's pages - the page code is untouched by
      this chapter; a UI swap is an owner product call outside the
      research frame). Effects per grants law 7: paths arrive as
      granted arguments, never resolved. The program tree/lists its
      prefix; tree/gets each item (a record tree per the records
      subsection) and decodes fields; answers board queries (open
      and done counts via MATH PRIMS - aggregates ride the prim per
      the F6 consequence while INVARIANT CHECKS stay pure: state is
      only open|done, counts re-foldable by replay); transitions an
      item state on the handed id and tree/puts the updated record
      back. Every boundary call journaled; the transcript is a run
      row like any other.

      LANDED: source scripts/dialect/todo-board.sabra (the fold /
      read half) + scripts/dialect/todo-transition.sabra (the write
      half: tree/get an item, flip state in-calculus, tree/put it
      back) + scripts/dialect/todo-open.sabra (the filter query:
      list-filter the open items, list-map their titles - the reach
      list-filter was cited for), driver scripts/dialect/
      bridge_probe.py, checked by acceptance 12.5.  All three halves
      journal tree/get, tree/put, tree/list, math/add and
      replay-verify.  BUILD NOTE: list-filter's first draft dispatched
      the wrong scrutinee (tree-case reads the PREDICATE's answer, not
      the accumulator); caught by the open-query before landing, fixed
      by regenerating the row - a reminder that the defs, unlike the
      reader sugar, are checked only by their corpus, so every v1.1
      def needs its exercised row (verify-8 law 2).
      The granted prefix travels WITHOUT a trailing slash (`todo-cal`,
      not `todo-cal/`): tree/list's prefix argument is a plain path
      that still selects the `todo-cal/*` slice, and the grant's
      path_prefix uses the same spelling. A trailing slash exposed a
      substrate inconsistency (tree/list used path validation where
      the HTTP surface used prefix validation); fixed inline and
      recorded in FINDINGS.md. The board namespace is todo-cal-v12 in
      the script so re-runs never collide with the host page.

      RECORD SHAPE is the port's first-class use of the new
      conventions: title (string), state (small nat or bool tag,
      make-time call), attribution (who as string, when as int via
      number literals). Exact field set pinned at make time by what
      the program writes - this paragraph is deliberately NOT the
      schema; the bridge source is.  LANDED schema: key 1 = state
      (%10 open | %0 done), key 2 = title (cstr string), key 3 = who
      (cstr string), key 4 = when (law-5 int, a number literal); the
      summary record is key 1 = open count, key 2 = done count.
      |))))
  (subsection acceptance
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Instrument: scripts/verify-12-dialect.sh over a live dev
      server (the shared verify-lib preamble; probes live where the
      other chapters keep theirs). Green means ALL hold:

      12.1 DESUGAR EQUALITY. Every shipped sugar form paired with its
          hand-written expansion: identical program hash, identical
          step counts, all three engines agreeing. Rows join the
          diff corpus (dialect_*): each number literal against its
          %-pin twin, each bracket list against its pair-chain twin,
          let against the lambda twin, each keyed literal against its
          [key-value value] twin; letrec (if shipped) against
          the rec-fix twin. This is the law that makes sugar unable
          to smuggle semantics.
      12.2 COMPAT. (a) every existing corpus source re-compiles to
          its recorded hash - the reader change is strictly
          additive; (b) grep audit: no stdlib name, corpus fixture
          or stored program source uses the newly reserved shapes
          ((let as a form head; -digit-run identifiers); (c)
          double-boot seeding still green, zero duplicates over
          53+v1.1 rows (verify-8 [8.1] re-run).
      12.3 READER SEAM. The landing commit's compiler diff touches
          sexp.ml ONLY (lexer + desugar + head reservation); Ir.ml,
          bracket.ml, interpreter/ byte-identical; the common/ codec
          beside Cstr is the only other core-side addition. A
          violation is F13, never a patch.
      12.4 FUEL TABLE (recorded at make time, both engines - the
          D-class instrument): bracket construction vs hand
          pair-chain (the zero-triage claim, measured); record
          get/upd over the port's own records; the KEY-DOMAIN rows
          (small-nat tags vs int keys vs cstr strings); bridge runs
          at 4/64/512 items under pinned fuel 1e7 - exact steps
          recorded beside the F-class cousins.
      12.5 TODO BRIDGE. The dogfood run: status normal; journal
          complete for every boundary call; replay identity green;
          deriv-sealed and deriv-check exit 0 - the ledger-bridge
          shape (verify-8 [8.5]) widened to a namespace-sized
          workload, invariants pure, aggregates on prims.
      12.6 BOOK HYGIENE. stdlib v1.1 rows in the manifest; corpus
          grows by the dialect rows without losing a single old
          entry; borge lint + report clean; the F13/F14 statuses, if
          they move, move in the same commit as the evidence.

      OUTCOMES 2026-10-02 - all six green (scripts/verify-12-dialect.sh
      is the live instrument):
      12.1 DESUGAR EQUALITY: 6/6 live pairs compile to identical
         ternary + size (brackets, [], 42, -7, let, nested brackets);
         corpus dialect_* rows add three-engine agreement (89/89
         including keyed_*/todo_flip).  v0.2: 5/5 compiler keyed tests
         + 3 corpus rows (dialect_keyed_literal, dialect_keyed_
         accessor, dialect_todo_flip) agree.
      12.2 COMPAT: bare 0 remains the leaf (0 1 0); the -<digits>
         reservation audit is clean; corpus intact and grown (89).
      12.3 READER SEAM: compiler delta since the dialect baseline is
         exactly compiler/lib/sexp.ml; interpreter/ byte-identical;
         the common/ codec (Tuna.Int_enc) is the only other core-side
         addition.  verify-8 [8.6] was widened to this law (the old
         blanket interpreter+compiler+common byte-check could not
         express a chapter whose whole point is a reader delta).
      12.4 FUEL TABLE: bracket compile cost equals the hand pair-chain
         (zero triage); rec-upd corpus 4,683 v0 steps; rec-get
         2,158 (present) / 2,321 (absent); rec-has 2,162; rec-val
         2,163; list-filter 286.  KEY DOMAIN (4-field lookup):
         small-nat tags 2,051 < int keys 4,418 < cstr strings 27,817.
      12.5 TODO BRIDGE: 2 open / 1 done, 7 journal rows (tree/list +
         six math/add), replay verified; transition (tree/get +
         tree/put) and open-query (list-filter + list-map) also
         replay-verified.  Source: scripts/dialect/todo-{board,
         transition,open}.sabra; driver scripts/dialect/bridge_probe.py.
         v0.2 CREATE call site (pure vocabulary): 64 steps, one
         journaled tree/put.
      12.6 BOOK HYGIENE: sabralib 76 defs (53 v1 + 8 v1.1 records +
         15 v0.2 prelude); dialect corpus rows present;
         borge lint/report clean. |))))
  (subsection pre-registered-failures
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      F13 SUGAR SEAM BREACH. TRIGGER: any v0.1 form cannot desugar
      into the reader's existing grammar; or a 12.1 row diverges on
      tree or steps; or any form pushes a change into Ir.ml,
      bracket.ml or interpreter/; or desugar multiplies compiled
      size against the hand twin (L6). THEN: the fold thesis fails
      for that form - DROP the form (never keep it by growing IR or
      the core), record in FINDINGS.md, and route the true need (if
      real) to a linking/pretty-printer chapter. This is the
      stdlib acceptance-6 law applied to the reader seam.

      F14 WRITABILITY CEILING. TRIGGER: the bridge cannot close at
      512 items under the pinned budget (fuel 1e7, default size
      cap) or blows TUNA_RUN_MAX_SECONDS into deadline_exceeded;
      or the compiled bridge program exceeds 100k nodes (the F5
      wire, carried from defs to programs); or the port cannot be
      completed without a prim family outside the pinned set
      (tree/*, value/*, math/*). THEN: construction sugar plus the
      record vocabulary did not buy package-scale writability -
      record with the port source and the 12.4 rows as evidence;
      the consequence is an evidence-named vocabulary or linking
      pass, NEVER more keywords adjudicated on taste. The owner's
      plain read of the finished port ('could a stranger write
      this?') is recorded beside the verdict either way, as input
      to that pass - deliberately not a trigger, to keep F14 as
      objective as the F9 gates.

      OUTCOMES 2026-10-02 (full record: FINDINGS.md).  F13 NOT
      triggered: the reader fold is total and checked (12.1).  F14
      OBSERVED at the 512-item target.  Measured board bridge under
      fuel 1e7 / size_cap 1e5:
         items   steps      journal   status
           4      71,833      9        normal
          16     287,189     33        normal
          64   1,148,613    129        normal
         256         128      1        normal + tree/list error
         512         128      1        normal + tree/list error
      The 256+/512 runs fail at the EFFECT, not the calculus: the
      prim `tree/list` caps its result at list_cap = 256 entries AND
      Prims.payload_cap = 65,536 ternary bytes, answering a journaled
      `Error' ('result exceeds the journal payload cap') that folds
      to an empty board.  So the ceiling is a HOST pagination gap,
      not a reader-sugar gap: package-scale boards need a windowed
      read (or a board sized to the cap) - an evidence-named follow-
      up, per the F14 THEN clause.  Step cost is also superlinear
      (1.15M at 64 items; rec-get/rec-upd fold costs above), which
      the same pass must price.  This is the correction of the
      todo-probe's verdict: the language could carry the ITEM LOGIC
      (12.5 green), it could not carry the WHOLE BOARD at unbounded
      size.  A vocabulary pass, not a syntax pass.|))))
 )
)
